    "description": "Commercial photography in Iran includes commissioned images of products, food, fashion, architecture, and industrial activity. Selected studio portfolios show photography operating alongside video, campaign planning, and social media production, with documented examples in Tehran and Mashhad.",
    "tags": ["Commercial photography", "Advertising", "Product photography", "Visual communication", "Creative industries"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:39:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:39:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Commercial Photography in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian commercial photography through documented product, food, fashion, architectural, and industrial studio portfolios."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Images commissioned to communicate",
        "content": "Commercial photography is best understood here through its commissioned purpose: presenting a product, place, service, or brand to an intended audience. Iranian studio portfolios reveal a broad field rather than a single visual style. Bartstudio separates architectural, industrial, advertising, restaurant, jewellery, portrait, and fashion work. SPIN describes product, architectural, spatial, and brand-oriented portrait photography. These categories identify different subjects and client needs; they are not interchangeable labels for attractive pictures. [s1][s2]\n\nThe sources also show why commercial photography belongs within content production. A photograph may be one component of a larger campaign involving moving images, copy, and publication. Reihanet Studio explicitly offers photography alongside video editing, campaign concepts, and social media management. Its service description documents an integrated offering, although it does not establish that every Iranian photographer works within an agency. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "subjects",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From a factory floor to a restaurant table",
        "content": "Bartstudio identifies itself as the joint practice of Ashkan Ghafari and Yasaman Khosravi, active in Tehran since 2018. Its portfolio includes industrial and mining work as well as architecture and advertising. This range makes an important distinction visible: commercial image-making is not confined to consumer fashion or small objects photographed in a studio. Buildings, production environments, and business facilities can also be commissioned subjects. [s1]\n\nSPIN provides a different view from Mashhad, a major city in northeastern Iran. Its photography portfolio names restaurant, catering, confectionery, and food-business projects, including work explicitly labelled for social media and Instagram. These examples establish activity outside the capital without supporting a ranking of cities or a claim about regional market shares. They also show that a local hospitality business can be a commercial photography client alongside larger brands. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "campaign-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A photograph within a campaign",
        "content": "Reihanet’s archive gives concrete examples of how subjects and outputs overlap. It describes fashion collection photography and campaign video for Adora, food photography and videography for Patiss Cafe Bakery, and advertising images of Alborz Ceramic tiles arranged on stairs and in still-life settings. These project descriptions show different ways of presenting goods: on a person, within a usable environment, or as part of a composed scene. [s3]\n\nThe same studio groups its services into production, campaigns, and account management. Its descriptions include concepts developed with the client before shooting, followed by content creation and publication-related work. The editorial implication is that evaluating a commercial image requires attention to its intended use as well as its appearance. A striking individual frame and a coherent sequence for a brand’s feed solve related but distinct communication problems. This interpretation follows the studio’s service structure; it is not evidence of campaign effectiveness. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-portfolios",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a portfolio can establish",
        "content": "A portfolio is valuable primary evidence of the work a studio chooses to present and the services it says it supplies. It is weaker evidence for typical prices, independently confirmed client satisfaction, or business outcomes. SPIN’s statements about conveying brand messages and Bartstudio’s service categories should therefore be read as professional self-descriptions rather than independent performance assessments. [s1][s2]\n\nTaken together, the examples reveal a varied commissioned practice spanning food businesses, built environments, industry, and consumer brands. They also caution against treating commercial photography as a synonym for documentary reporting: these portfolios organise images around client communication. The clearest reading of the field starts with the subject, the commission, and the intended publication context, while keeping claims about sales impact or national scale separate from the available evidence. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
